\section{Superpotential and the excluded cases}

With a superpotential, case (3) requires a field charged under the free node to sit above $r=2/3$ at the fixed point of the other node. Case (5) requires a field charged under one node only whose $R$-charge is higher where its node interacts than where it is free, and whether a superpotential can arrange this is open.

With a superpotential, the exclusion of case (3) can be evaded only by a constraint that forces a field charged under the free node above $r=2/3$. The only way around the exclusions of cases (3) and (5) is a superpotential constraint that forces a field above $r=2/3$.

